<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Sharifate of Mecca</h1><p class="st-meta">967 CE – 1916 CE (approx.) · capital Mecca</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Nearly a thousand years of rule by the Hashemite Sharifs, descendants of the Prophet, over Mecca, Medina and the pilgrimage.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 967 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jafar-ibn-muhammad-al-hasani">Ja'far ibn Muhammad</a>, a descendant of al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasan-ibn-ali">Hasan ibn Ali</a>, took control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> rose in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The Sharifs, as these Hasanid nobles were known, then governed the holy city under whichever power controlled Egypt. In 1201 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatada-ibn-idris">Qatada ibn Idris</a> founded the line that ruled until 1925.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Sharifs kept order in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> and the pilgrimage roads, collected dues from pilgrims and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeddah">Jeddah's</a> port and balanced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes. Their suzerains, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> and, from 1517, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, sent annual grain and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>. Family feuds over succession were frequent, and the overlord often chose between rival sharifs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-numayy-ii">Abu Numayy II</a> secured a long reign in the 16th century.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecca">Mecca</a> from 1803 to 1813. Ottoman control tightened in the 19th century, and in 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussein-bin-ali-king-of-hejaz">Sharif Hussein</a> ibn Ali launched the Arab Revolt and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-hejaz">Kingdom of Hejaz</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sharifate-of-mecca.jpg" alt="Sharifate of Mecca" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Alexis Hubert Jaillot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estats_de_l%27Empire_du_Grand_Seigneur_des_Turcs_-_1695_-_Estats_du_cherif_de_la_Mecque.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>967 CE – 1916 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mecca</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Qatada ibn Idris, Abu Numayy II, Hussein ibn Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Islam (Zaydi, later Sunni)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Emirate of Mecca, Sharifs of Mecca, Hejaz Sharifate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1442&amp;focus=sharifate-of-mecca" aria-label="Sharifate of Mecca on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5750 1386 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sharifate of Mecca lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6070 1642L5811 1337M6070 1642L5811 1337M6070 1642L5815 1335M6070 1642L5709 970M6070 1642L6237 1523M6070 1642L6070 1642"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5811 1337h0M5811 1337h0M5815 1335h0M5709 970h0M6237 1523h0M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6070 1642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6070 1642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Mecca. <a href="/atlas?year=1442&amp;focus=sharifate-of-mecca">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
